{
  "bottom_line": "ESTIMATOR_BIASED: the frozen N-001 cumulative estimator's error at the primary window is {'truth_1.85': 0.15370030089772702, 'truth_2.05': 0.11155053953974892} for true exponents [1.85, 2.05]. The stored production value 1.9200860948994347 is not edited. The deviation from the Fisher value is in the downward direction at every window under every estimator, so the escalation direction is unaffected even where the numeric value is not trusted.",
  "claim_guards": [
    "The frozen N-001 production number tau = 1.92009 is not edited, replaced, or deleted. A bias-corrected value is a separately labelled secondary estimate at most.",
    "The production runner is not modified. The estimators are re-implemented and validated in this isolated directory.",
    "A measured bias on synthetic data is not automatically a valid correction for real percolation data, whose local curvature is not the curvature of the synthetic constructions. That is exactly what the two-truth test decides.",
    "Finding a bias does not overturn the escalation direction. It weakens the numeric value, and must not be reported as though it had removed the deviation.",
    "No physics claim, no novelty claim, and nothing here bears on any other experiment.",
    "If the bias turns out to be small or transferable-absent, the stored tau stands and this experiment is a null result reported as such."
  ],
  "disclosure": "INFORMED, and labelled as such. This experiment was conceived after exploratory work already showed that the N-001 cumulative estimator returns values well above a known input exponent on synthetic power laws. The exploratory range of the bias, the three weighting variants, and the off-by-one survivor-count hypothesis have all already been seen. What is fixed here in advance is the validation design, the decision rule for whether a bias correction may be applied to the real data at all, and the set of truths and windows to be tested. In particular the decisive question addressed here - whether the bias depends on the true exponent, and therefore whether any bias correction is transferable - had not been answered.",
  "fisher_tau": 2.0549450549450547,
